From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Morinaga <me@gustavomorinaga.dev>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 11:44:40 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Vulkan: the internal resolution follows the window and the
 scale setting while running

Symptom: the frame stayed at the size the first window gave. A game started in a 1280x720 window and switched to
3440x1440, borderless or fullscreen on the PC page drew its scene at 1280x720 and stretched it; the Internal
Resolution setting changed nothing until the next start. The same for Alt+Enter and a window resized by hand.

Cause: vk_fit_scale ran once, after the device was made. vk_apply_display (on the window's thread) only resizes
the window, and the present only remakes the swapchain: the scale (s_rscale_x/y), the display image, its depth
and readback, every engine colour and depth target, and the pixel programs that read the window position (their
1/scale baked in at vk_eng_pipeline_create) kept the start's size.

Fix: at the end of each present (vk_cb_present, after the window's present) vk_rescale_check works out the size
wanted now: with PS3RECOMP_VK_SCALE_FIT, rsx_fit_scale (the arithmetic vk_fit_scale used, now shared and
host-tested in rsx_fit_scale.h) on the swapchain's extent, times the scale setting; without it, the scale set by
the new rsx_vulkan_set_scale (any thread). When that differs from the frame's size and was the same at the last
present (a window dragged larger is not remade at every one), vk_rescale, with the device idle:

- makes every engine colour and depth target again at the new scale under the same handle, the old picture
  blitted into it (linear for colour; depth blitted where its format allows, else cleared to the far plane), and
  the views of it again (vk_eng_surface_view); copies the engine owns (colour snapshots, depth as colour) that
  are free go, one in use keeps its size until it is given back; the depth-as-colour descriptor sets are written
  again at their next use (a generation count), and grow their buffer instead of failing;
- makes the display image, its depth, the readback buffer and the vtable path's framebuffer again;
- drops the framebuffers and the scratch depth, and the VkPipelines and pixel modules of the pipelines whose
  pixel program reads the window position: those keep their source (wpos_ps) and get their modules again at
  their next draw (vk_eng_pipe_wpos), the velocity variant's too. The SPIR-V and pipeline caches are keyed on
  the program text, so a scale already met is read from disk;
- under a temporal upscaler (DLSS, FSR 3.1): the output is the new frame size and the scene the upscaler's render
  size for it; the display buffers get their own images back, the outputs, descriptor caches and depth views go,
  the history starts over, rsx_temporal_config takes the new scale, and DLSS's feature or FSR 3.1's context is
  released (rsx_dlss_drop, rsx_fsr3_drop) to be made again at the next evaluate. Its motion and depth images, the
  velocity target, MLAA's buffers and FSR 1's images already followed the drawn size.

The log gets "window WxH: frame rendered at WxH (rescaled: N targets, M pipelines)" and, with an upscaler, the
"temporal: ... scene drawn at" line again.

ENHANCEMENT: the PS3 game has one output size. Written from this backend's own code; nothing taken from another
project.
